From 4982be077a8d75688a108554c37fe9f9b2b55eac Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Corios00 <52224974+Corios00@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 16 Nov 2026 18:30:43 -0600
Subject: [PATCH] The perf log names the systems a slow frame spent its time
 in, and the Lua collector's pauses

Elwynn Forest runs CPU-bound on the main thread (15 ms a frame, 3-5 frames a second at 70-90 ms)
and the perf log could say only that. Under each ten-second summary it now writes:

- the 8 costliest ECS systems by wall ms a frame, with the main thread's share and the most one
  frame spent in each;
- the worst frame: its length, the Lua collections in it and the ms they took, the main thread's
  ms inside systems, and its 8 costliest systems;
- the frames over 33 ms: how many, how many held a Lua collection (and its ms), and the systems
  most often in their top 3, with their average ms there;
- the Lua heap, the collections this window, their ms and the most in one frame.

Per-system time comes from `bevy_ecs`'s `trace` spans, one around each system run and one around
applying its commands, made once per system; `benilla_world::system_times` is a tracing layer
beside the crash log's ring that times them into one slot per system (a relaxed atomic add per run)
and leaves out a system that runs whole schedules inside itself (`Main::run_main`, the fixed-step
runner). `debug` comes with it so a player build (no `dev`) names its systems. The Lua VM times its
full collections, each a stop-the-world pass on the main thread. `WOW_SYSTEM_TIMES=0` turns the
timing off; the Lua lines stay.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
